# Target C-II — the whole pentagon sea forces host escape or a linear contact-flex space

**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` network reduction
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II train-measure branch
**Dependencies:** audited stopped-train endpoint geometry; audited exceptional-face closure matrix; uniform pentagon sea through `t<=2.02`
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_pentagon_sea_escape_or_flex.py`
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The high-`t` pentagon theorem forces linearly many source-empty bounded pentagons through the whole range `t<=2.02`. Every side of every such pentagon is a terminal occurrence of exactly one complete stopped train. This includes singleton trains: an edge incident to no strict rhombus is itself one complete stopped train. The audited no-bounded-return theorem excludes a nontrivial train from terminating twice on one bounded face, and bounded singleton loops are excluded by the endpoint geometry.

Let `\mathcal P_5` be the full set of bounded pentagonal faces and put `P=P_5`. Let

- `E_int` be the number of complete stopped trains whose two terminal faces are both pentagons;
- `E_esc` be the number of complete stopped trains with exactly one pentagonal terminal face.

Occurrence-counting the five sides of every pentagon gives the exact handshake

\[
\boxed{5P=2E_{int}+E_{esc}.}
\tag{PE.1}
\]

## 1. Internal pentagon trains force train-rate kernel

Restrict the canonical exceptional-face closure matrix `C_face` to the `2P` scalar rows belonging to pentagonal faces and the `E_int` columns belonging to pentagon-to-pentagon trains. Call the resulting matrix `C_{5,int}`.

Every internal train has both stopping faces in `\mathcal P_5`; hence its full `C_face` column is zero on all exceptional-face rows outside this set. Therefore each vector in `ker C_{5,int}` extends by zero to a global train-rate vector in `ker C_face`.

Since `rank C_{5,int}<=2P`,

\[
\dim\ker C_{5,int}\ge E_{int}-2P.
\]

Using (PE.1),

\[
\boxed{
\dim\ker C_{5,int}
\ge {P-E_{esc}\over2}.
}
\tag{PE.2}
\]

The global infinitesimal rotation occupies at most one dimension of this supported kernel. Via the audited train-rate/contact-flex isomorphism, the non-Euclidean contact-flex dimension generated by internal pentagon trains therefore satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\dim\mathcal V_5
\ge {P-E_{esc}\over2}-1.
}
\tag{PE.3}
\]

The localization is in train-rate coordinates, not necessarily in vertex support.

## 2. Escape-or-flex Pareto frontier

For every fixed `0<=eta<=1`,

\[
E_{esc}<\eta P
\Longrightarrow
\dim\mathcal V_5>{1-\eta\over2}P-1.
\tag{PE.4}
\]

The symmetric choice `eta=1/2` gives

\[
\boxed{
E_{esc}\ge{P\over2}
\quad\text{or}\quad
\dim\mathcal V_5\ge{P\over4}-1.
}
\tag{PE.5}
\]

This is unconditional. It does not use the singleton/rhombic Branch-S/Branch-R split and it does not require fully rhombic pentagons.

## 3. Certified high-`t` rows

The uniform all-train theorem gives

\[
{P_5\over D}\ge p_5(T)-o(1),
\qquad
p_5(T)=\pi+C_{707}-{5T\over2}.
\]

Combining this with (PE.5), the exact outward certificate gives

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
T & P_5/D & E_{esc}/D & \dim\mathcal V_5/D\\ \hline
1.880 & >0.3539 & >0.1769\ \text{or} & >0.0884\\
1.900 & >0.3039 & >0.1519\ \text{or} & >0.0759\\
1.920 & >0.2539 & >0.1269\ \text{or} & >0.0634\\
1.960 & >0.1539 & >0.0769\ \text{or} & >0.0384\\
2.000 & >0.0539 & >0.0269\ \text{or} & >0.0134\\
2.010 & >0.0289 & >0.0144\ \text{or} & >0.0072\\
2.020 & >0.0039 & >0.0019\ \text{or} & >0.0009
\end{array}
\tag{PE.6}
\]

The `-1` in (PE.5) vanishes after normalization by `D`.

## 4. Relation to mixed rigidity

In the flex alternative, canonical mixed infinitesimal rigidity forces the actual-diameter derivative map to be injective on `\mathcal V_5`: a non-Euclidean contact flex cannot vanish on every actual diameter row. Thus a linear internal pentagon flex space requires a linear-dimensional diameter-blocking image.

This does not yet contradict the Hopf--Pannwitz linear diameter-edge budget. It does, however, turn the high-`t` pentagon sea into one of two global mechanisms:

```text
escape branch:
    linearly many complete trains leave the pentagon host set;

flex branch:
    pentagon-to-pentagon trains alone generate a linear-dimensional
    non-Euclidean contact-flex space that the diameter graph must block.
```

The escape branch can next be split between outer-face terminals and degree-`>=6` bounded hosts. The flex branch can next be attacked with exact mixed-rigidity and diameter-thrackle rank machinery.

**Disposition:** proved exact unconditional pentagon-sea escape-or-flex reduction through `t<=2.02`. C-II and Level I remain open.
